00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:05.683
of how basic microscopes kind of work.

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:11.820
And so here we have this
really dusty old microscope

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:15.770
that no one's really used in the lab.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:20.770
And this is kind of the
basic light microscopes

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:23.460
that you would initially
see if you kind of took

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:27.860
like a beginning biology course
in college, like in a lab.

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:30.680
And just to kind of really
quickly go over what the parts

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:34.240
of the microscope are
because these are basics

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:37.630
that every microscope no
matter how advanced you get,

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:40.670
basically have and how
it normally functions.

00:08:40.670 --> 00:08:43.340
And so over here you have your objectives

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:45.550
and so these are the lenses
that you will actually see

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:47.740
through and put your eyes
on to be able to look

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:48.723
at your specimen.

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:52.670
Over here is the stage of the microscope.

00:08:52.670 --> 00:08:56.170
And so I just have a
standard microscope slide.

00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:58.790
So usually you'll mount whatever
you want to look at here

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:02.070
whether it's like a tissue
sample, like a drop of pond water

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:03.670
or whatever else you want to look at

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:05.150
that has really small things.

00:09:05.150 --> 00:09:08.910
You'd put them on your microscope
slide and you'd slide it

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:13.170
on the stage so that your
sample goes right over

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:14.860
where this opening is.

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:16.390
And you'll see right through the opening,

00:09:16.390 --> 00:09:18.120
you'll see this other kind of a lens,

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:19.640
it's called an objective.

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:22.440
And so let me go underneath
the stage for you guys.

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:26.150
And so here is where you
have these objectives

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:29.440
And these objectives are
what actually magnifies

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:33.420
and allows you to be able
to see like your specimen

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:38.290
at a size that you can see,
visualize with your eye.

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:40.850
And see that they have different

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:44.800
kind of magnification abilities.

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:47.800
So here, when it says 40 X,
that means it's able to magnify

00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:50.970
something 40 times larger
than it actually is.

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:54.420
And then 20 and then this should be a 10 X

00:09:56.170 --> 00:09:58.790
And so, it's through these objectives

00:09:58.790 --> 00:10:01.930
that you're actually able
to see a bigger image

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:03.160
of your sample.

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:05.300
But then what else you
need with a microscope

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:08.410
is that you actually need
to have a light source.

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:10.610
And that's what everything on top here is.

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:13.920
So this is a really, really
strong lamp that then shines

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:18.260
a whole bunch of light onto
your sample and then that allows

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:21.140
enough light through into the objectives

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:22.730
and then you're able to view it

00:10:22.730 --> 00:10:27.370
through these objective lenses.

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:29.770
And so that's kind of the standard setup

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:33.010
of what a typical light
or bright field microscope

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:35.494
is the term I'll probably be using is.

00:10:35.494 --> 00:10:38.670
And it will probably be the
first microscope that you guys

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:39.670
would have experience with

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:41.650
if you want to go into microscopy.

00:10:41.650 --> 00:10:43.580
Oh and then this box
here is just the camera,

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:44.863
so you can take pictures.

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:50.670
And so that was kind of the
first microscope I ever used

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:51.560
back in college.

