to do another poll question.

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:44.950
So with that kind of
bright field microscope

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:46.820
that I just had, showed you guys,

00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:49.740
what do you think the smallest
thing you could possibly see

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:50.573
with it is?

00:14:13.630 --> 00:14:16.293
When I do these polls I kind
of feel like I'm on jeopardy.

00:14:17.670 --> 00:14:20.040
- [Jeanne] Rochelle, that was
like literally the thought

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:20.873
in my head.

00:14:20.873 --> 00:14:21.706
I was like, we should think

00:14:21.706 --> 00:14:23.413
about the jeopardy music for this.

00:14:25.150 --> 00:14:26.063
- Very in tune.

00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:30.283
Okay, great.

00:14:30.283 --> 00:14:32.960
Only a portion of people
voted, I'll share results.

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:35.680
Oh, well, we have a wide range of things

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:39.600
that we potentially could
look at and I'll preface this,

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:41.330
a caveat that I think someone,

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:44.240
like poll questions do kind
of have a bit of a trick to it

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:48.680
because there, depending
on what you kind of see

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:51.420
and how you have your microscope set up,

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:54.690
you can see a wide range of
things down to even potentially

00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:57.380
a singular protein, depending
on how big the protein

00:14:57.380 --> 00:14:58.640
you want to look at is.

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:00.690
But we typically use that microscope

00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:01.640
that I just showed you guys.

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:04.350
We usually use it just
to look kind of at cells

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:06.830
and with the right stain, you
maybe you can start seeing

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:09.060
a cell's organelles such
as like the nucleus,

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:10.660
which is one of the bigger ones.

00:15:12.990 --> 00:15:16.970
And so now I'll get into
more of the presentation

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:17.803
I have set up.

00:15:20.450 --> 00:15:24.240
Before I do kind of more
practical demonstrations.

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:27.810
And so again, just reminding you guys

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:31.010
that here's what we can
see with the naked eye,

00:15:31.010 --> 00:15:33.180
here is using that bright field microscope

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:34.220
I just showed you.

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:36.760
The kind of scale that we
can start to see things at

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:37.700
using that microscope.

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:40.770
So really just kind of cells,
maybe some smaller cells

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:42.453
and maybe like a nucleus or so.

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:46.740
And then here is just an example
of like the kind of images

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:49.870
that you really do get from
using bright field microscopy.

00:15:49.870 --> 00:15:53.380
And so you can already begin
to see, pointer options,

00:15:56.270 --> 00:15:58.520
that you can see individual cells.

00:15:58.520 --> 00:16:01.350
And this is actually really
important for a lot of biology

00:16:01.350 --> 00:16:03.640
because you can already
get a lot of information

00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:07.140
just by looking at how, what
do cells even look like?

00:16:07.140 --> 00:16:08.450
Like just from this straight off

00:16:08.450 --> 00:16:10.890
I can already kind of
tell like all of these

00:16:10.890 --> 00:16:13.760
are actually oligodendrocytes
and you kind of know that

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:15.420
because of their branching off pattern.

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:17.690
And I know that they're really
healthy oligodendrocytes

00:16:17.690 --> 00:16:19.620
because they're branching really well

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:21.720
and they're not rounded
up and kind of sad.

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:23.390
And so you already get
a lot of information

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:25.780
about the cells biology this way.

00:16:25.780 --> 00:16:29.270
But you can still, like you
can see individual cells

00:16:29.270 --> 00:16:31.390
but you can't really
see much detail to it.

00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:33.250
And so actually what a lot of people do

00:16:33.250 --> 00:16:34.810
when they do bright field microscopy

00:16:34.810 --> 00:16:37.820
is they actually have
the addition of stains.

00:16:37.820 --> 00:16:40.530
And so depending on what
kind of stains you use

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:42.670
you can start to get a
whole bunch more detail

00:16:42.670 --> 00:16:43.900
and start understanding more

00:16:43.900 --> 00:16:46.450
about how a cell is actually organized.

00:16:46.450 --> 00:16:50.280
And so here is just an
example picture of a section

00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:53.440
of plant tissue that
they've stained in green.

00:16:53.440 --> 00:16:55.690
I think the cell walls
of the plant tissue.

00:16:55.690 --> 00:16:57.450
So each of these should
be one of the cells

00:16:57.450 --> 00:16:59.250
and then in green, kind of the cell wall

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:01.730
and then in red they
stain for the nucleus.

00:17:01.730 --> 00:17:03.540
So you can see where that is.

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:07.060
And you can even begin to see
these other little structures.

00:17:07.060 --> 00:17:10.270
These are all the little sub
compartments of the cell.

00:17:10.270 --> 00:17:12.450
And so even though bright field microscopy

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:14.800
doesn't let you see super super small

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:16.550
down to like the atom size.

00:17:16.550 --> 00:17:19.670
You can still get a lot
of information on things

00:17:19.670 --> 00:17:22.240
that you wouldn't be able
to tell are happening

00:17:22.240 --> 00:17:23.763
just with your naked eye.

00:17:24.765 --> 00:17:29.030
But still the question is, we
can now kind of get a handle

00:17:29.030 --> 00:17:31.690
of like cells themselves.

00:17:31.690 --> 00:17:35.900
But what if we were more
interested in more details,

00:17:35.900 --> 00:17:39.060
like all these cells kind
of look the same to me.

00:17:39.060 --> 00:17:42.410
So I can't really tell what
is what and what's happening.

00:17:42.410 --> 00:17:45.080
And is there a way using
microscopy, we're going to be able

00:17:45.080 --> 00:17:48.010
to differentiate like
two different cell types

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:51.680
or like be able to even start
visualizing finer detail

00:17:51.680 --> 00:17:53.090
within the cell?

00:17:53.090 --> 00:17:55.590
And I put that question out
there and the answer is, yes

00:17:55.590 --> 00:17:57.290
of course, there is a way to do that.

00:17:57.290 --> 00:17:59.820
And that is through using
fluorescence microscopy,

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:02.060
which some of you have
said that you have heard of

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:03.660
or are familiar with.

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:07.570
And so for those of you
who aren't familiar,

00:18:07.570 --> 00:18:09.650
fluorescence microscopy, I
just wanted to kind of give

00:18:09.650 --> 00:18:12.120
a brief background on what
this fluorescence mean

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:13.750
and how does it work.

00:18:13.750 --> 00:18:18.750
And so you have your sample
that you want to look at

00:18:18.940 --> 00:18:23.160
and you have these
proteins within your sample

00:18:23.160 --> 00:18:24.550
that potentially you want to study

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:26.210
and want to know what they do.

00:18:26.210 --> 00:18:28.440
And I have here just kind of a diagram

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:30.510
of like a basic unknown protein.

00:18:30.510 --> 00:18:32.740
And I have it in black to
kind of signify, right?

00:18:32.740 --> 00:18:34.240
That like, you can't really see it.

00:18:34.240 --> 00:18:37.350
You can't really tell, differentiate it

00:18:37.350 --> 00:18:39.810
between other proteins around in the cell.

00:18:39.810 --> 00:18:42.800
And so it's just kind of
floating there mysteriously.

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:44.623
And so if I do want to visualize it,

00:18:44.623 --> 00:18:47.240
what you can do is you add these things

00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:51.970
that will specifically find
this protein and bind onto it.

00:18:51.970 --> 00:18:56.680
And then that thing will have
something called a fluorophore

00:18:56.680 --> 00:18:59.920
and fluorophore is kind of a fancy word,

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:02.440
but really all you have to
worry about or know about

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:05.260
what a fluorophore is is
that it's just something

00:19:05.260 --> 00:19:07.613
that will emit a certain color of light,

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:11.210
once you shine light onto it.

00:19:11.210 --> 00:19:13.970
And so you have this
fluorophore that then finds

00:19:13.970 --> 00:19:18.450
your protein of interest and
you have it bound onto it.

00:19:18.450 --> 00:19:22.210
And then you go and you
shine a light source on it.

00:19:22.210 --> 00:19:24.310
Just kind of like how I
said that the bright field

00:19:24.310 --> 00:19:27.480
microscope works with its lamp source,

00:19:27.480 --> 00:19:29.900
but in this case your light is a laser

00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:32.100
and you shine this laser
onto the fluorophore

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:34.430
and then what the fluorophore
will do is it will actually

00:19:34.430 --> 00:19:36.810
emit its own specific colored lights.

00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:39.790
So in this case, green,
and then you can be able

00:19:39.790 --> 00:19:41.790
to visualize and detect that light.

00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:44.710
And then you're like, okay,
every time I see a green signal

00:19:44.710 --> 00:19:47.260
that means this protein is there.

00:19:47.260 --> 00:19:49.340
And what's really cool about fluorescence,

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:51.650
is that you can do this

00:19:51.650 --> 00:19:53.620
with a whole bunch of different proteins.

00:19:53.620 --> 00:19:57.080
And so all of these are
four different proteins

00:19:57.080 --> 00:19:59.480
that you want to be able
to visualize in your cell

00:19:59.480 --> 00:20:01.680
and you can go through
and you can tag each one

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:02.840
with its own different color.

00:20:02.840 --> 00:20:05.300
So like this protein I'll tag with red,

00:20:05.300 --> 00:20:08.620
this one I'll tag with yellow,
this one I'll tag with blue.

00:20:08.620 --> 00:20:11.780
And then you can look at a sample in total

00:20:11.780 --> 00:20:15.630
and you can be like, okay, I
see, like all the green spots

00:20:15.630 --> 00:20:18.810
are clumped up here, so I know
that's where this protein is.

00:20:18.810 --> 00:20:20.070
And then I have some red and blue

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:21.790
that seemed to be
interacting with each other.

00:20:21.790 --> 00:20:24.680
So I know these two proteins
interact with each other.

00:20:24.680 --> 00:20:27.070
And you can just get so
much information then

00:20:27.970 --> 00:20:30.720
just, you know, in one tissue section

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:33.543
and you can be able to
tell so much detail.

00:20:33.543 --> 00:20:35.760
And so I talked about
it a lot in the abstract

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:38.320
and now I wanted to show
you guys like a real

00:20:38.320 --> 00:20:41.840
fluorescent image that
I actually collected

00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:43.430
when I was at the NIH.

00:20:43.430 --> 00:20:46.840
And so what you see here
is actually a section

00:20:46.840 --> 00:20:50.620
of a human liver liver
sample that I then stained

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:53.410
with all of these different colors.

00:20:53.410 --> 00:20:56.570
And then was looking at
it to get an understanding

00:20:56.570 --> 00:20:59.000
of kind of like the
anatomy of a human liver.

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:00.800
And so this has a lot of things going on.

00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:04.930
So here's just a more cleaned
up version of that image.

00:21:04.930 --> 00:21:09.930
And what you see here is in
pink, actually I'm staining

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:14.400
for the vessels within the liver.

00:21:14.400 --> 00:21:17.810
And this is kind of where these
vessels kind of allow fluid

00:21:17.810 --> 00:21:19.400
to like move in and out of the liver.

00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:24.130
And it helps the liver be able
to do its detoxing function

00:21:24.130 --> 00:21:26.010
that we all know the
liver's important for.

00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:29.180
And then here in yellow,
what you're seeing stained

00:21:29.180 --> 00:21:31.610
is actually blood vessels in the liver.

00:21:31.610 --> 00:21:35.790
And then in green, the green
also staining blood vessels,

00:21:35.790 --> 00:21:37.560
it also has kind of staining neurons.

00:21:37.560 --> 00:21:39.730
So you can even start to
see where all the nerves

00:21:39.730 --> 00:21:41.380
in your liver are.

00:21:41.380 --> 00:21:44.730
And so just right away,
just from this one picture,

00:21:44.730 --> 00:21:47.350
you get so much information
of like, oh, this is how

00:21:47.350 --> 00:21:51.040
all the vessels in pink
look like within a liver.

00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:53.920
And here's how the blood vessels
kind of position themselves

00:21:53.920 --> 00:21:56.023
in your liver section and et cetera.

00:21:56.910 --> 00:21:59.730
And so this is just kind of a static image

00:21:59.730 --> 00:22:01.910
with a whole bunch of different
colors that you can look at

00:22:01.910 --> 00:22:05.270
and get a lot of, kind of
positioning information

00:22:05.270 --> 00:22:08.260
about in whatever sample
you're interested in.

00:22:08.260 --> 00:22:10.240
But what's also really
cool about fluorescence

00:22:10.240 --> 00:22:14.320
is that you can totally use
it in like a living animal.

00:22:14.320 --> 00:22:15.773
And so, oh,

00:22:17.370 --> 00:22:18.880
sorry.

