00:27:36.300 --> 00:27:38.980
- Yeah, yeah, no, that is great.

00:27:38.980 --> 00:27:42.020
I didn't want to get too
much into the details

00:27:42.020 --> 00:27:45.110
between the different types of
ways you can tag fluorescents

00:27:45.110 --> 00:27:47.360
but I think that's amazing
that you guys are reading

00:27:47.360 --> 00:27:48.900
and learning about GFP.

00:27:48.900 --> 00:27:52.800
Right, so GFP as you all probably know,

00:27:52.800 --> 00:27:54.450
originally discovered in jellyfish.

00:27:54.450 --> 00:27:55.563
So thank you to them.

00:27:56.640 --> 00:27:59.700
And it is actually, it is itself a protein

00:27:59.700 --> 00:28:02.030
that gets naturally expressed.

00:28:02.030 --> 00:28:04.870
And what's really cool
about GFP is that scientists

00:28:04.870 --> 00:28:08.260
have figured out a way to
be able to take that GFP

00:28:08.260 --> 00:28:11.110
which is fluorescent by itself and make it

00:28:11.110 --> 00:28:13.920
so that you kind of smoosh it
in with one of these proteins,

00:28:13.920 --> 00:28:15.930
potentially that you're interested in

00:28:15.930 --> 00:28:20.340
and get it so that when this
protein gets made in the cell

00:28:20.340 --> 00:28:22.850
it also has a GFP attached to it.

00:28:22.850 --> 00:28:24.710
And then you're able to kind of look at it

00:28:24.710 --> 00:28:26.419
under the microscope.

00:28:26.419 --> 00:28:30.610
And so that's one way of
tagging with fluorescence.

00:28:30.610 --> 00:28:33.270
What I have shown right
now is actually using

00:28:33.270 --> 00:28:35.190
things called antibodies.

00:28:35.190 --> 00:28:39.710
And so these aren't this
kind of fluorophore antibody

00:28:39.710 --> 00:28:40.980
picture I made.

00:28:40.980 --> 00:28:43.350
That's not, that's something you add,

00:28:43.350 --> 00:28:45.730
you as a scientist will
add into your sample.

00:28:45.730 --> 00:28:47.560
And then that will find its protein

00:28:47.560 --> 00:28:51.440
that it will bind to and recognize.

00:28:51.440 --> 00:28:53.780
And then that's how you
look at the fluorescence.

00:28:53.780 --> 00:28:55.560
And so that's kind of the difference.

00:28:55.560 --> 00:28:57.950
One kind of can get expressed with it,

00:28:57.950 --> 00:28:59.830
the other one is something you add in.

00:28:59.830 --> 00:29:02.923
But they both kind of give
you the same information.

00:29:07.596 --> 00:29:08.429
Okay, great.

00:29:08.429 --> 00:29:11.040
I mean, that's a great question

00:29:11.040 --> 00:29:12.940
and also a great topic to learn about.

00:29:15.080 --> 00:29:18.043
So that's fluorescence microscopy

00:29:18.043 --> 00:29:21.180
but then I wanted to
introduce one final version

00:29:21.180 --> 00:29:24.860
of microscopy, which is
actually what's behind me here,

00:29:24.860 --> 00:29:28.680
and that's single molecule microscopy.

00:29:28.680 --> 00:29:33.390
And so here's just again,
another snapshot of that website

00:29:33.390 --> 00:29:37.150
I showed you guys in the
beginning of different sizes.

00:29:37.150 --> 00:29:39.930
And here, I want to say
that the microscopy,

00:29:39.930 --> 00:29:42.900
we were originally
talking about microscopy

00:29:42.900 --> 00:29:45.690
that can look at things kind
of at the single cell level.

00:29:45.690 --> 00:29:47.700
And then now we're
going to go even smaller

00:29:47.700 --> 00:29:50.920
and go into single proteins
and start to be able to look at

00:29:50.920 --> 00:29:54.100
single proteins and see what they do.

00:29:54.100 --> 00:29:58.550
And so single molecule force microscopy,

00:29:58.550 --> 00:30:00.130
this might get a little convoluted.

00:30:00.130 --> 00:30:03.490
So totally stop me if I'm
unclear or it's kinda hard

00:30:03.490 --> 00:30:04.830
to follow along.

00:30:04.830 --> 00:30:08.870
But I wanted to introduce kind of the idea

00:30:08.870 --> 00:30:10.800
of a pulling force.

00:30:10.800 --> 00:30:13.970
And so we've all hopefully
have played tug of war

00:30:13.970 --> 00:30:16.390
or have heard of tug of war, right?

00:30:16.390 --> 00:30:19.940
Where you have two things
in between two people

00:30:19.940 --> 00:30:22.740
and you're each holding onto
the side and you're pulling.

00:30:22.740 --> 00:30:26.420
And you think about what you're
actually doing to that rope

00:30:26.420 --> 00:30:28.220
is you're putting energy into it, right?

00:30:28.220 --> 00:30:30.060
To kind of pull it apart.

00:30:30.060 --> 00:30:32.530
And if you have originally,
this like a really

00:30:32.530 --> 00:30:33.550
really tiny rope,

00:30:33.550 --> 00:30:36.280
that's really like like
a string basically.

00:30:36.280 --> 00:30:38.210
And both of you are pulling really hard.

00:30:38.210 --> 00:30:40.510
You probably can pull and
snap that string apart

00:30:40.510 --> 00:30:41.800
really easily.

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:44.130
But now if you consider, if
you have a rope kind of more

00:30:44.130 --> 00:30:47.270
of what I have shown in my picture here,

00:30:47.270 --> 00:30:50.730
that's like a really hefty
rope and that's really strong.

00:30:50.730 --> 00:30:53.600
You can pull a lot but it's
going to take a lot more energy

00:30:53.600 --> 00:30:55.310
potentially a lot more people actually

00:30:55.310 --> 00:30:57.203
to even be able to break that apart.

00:30:58.050 --> 00:31:01.740
And why am I talking about
pulling ropes and like energy

00:31:01.740 --> 00:31:04.777
and force right now when we
were talking about microscopy?

00:31:04.777 --> 00:31:07.300
It's because you can
actually use a microscope

00:31:07.300 --> 00:31:11.640
to kind of measure how much
energy you have to put in

00:31:11.640 --> 00:31:13.540
to pull something, to be
able to pull it apart.

00:31:13.540 --> 00:31:15.990
And that actually is really
really helpful for understanding

00:31:15.990 --> 00:31:17.950
how proteins work.

00:31:17.950 --> 00:31:21.790
And so basically if you
kind of think about this

00:31:21.790 --> 00:31:26.010
like pulling tug of war image
and then kind of move it

00:31:26.010 --> 00:31:29.310
over onto this diagram
I've shown here is that

00:31:29.310 --> 00:31:31.960
one of the really cool
things about lasers and light

00:31:31.960 --> 00:31:35.970
is that it can actually
interact with physical objects.

00:31:35.970 --> 00:31:38.420
And so if you have a strong enough laser

00:31:38.420 --> 00:31:41.630
you can actually trap a
bead, a plastic bead in it

00:31:41.630 --> 00:31:43.830
and be able to move
that plastic bead around

00:31:43.830 --> 00:31:45.620
just using the power of light.

00:31:45.620 --> 00:31:48.740
And so think about your lasers
and that bead you've trapped

00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:52.560
as like the hands on this
rope that are pulling.

00:31:52.560 --> 00:31:55.230
And then now that you have
these beads that you trapped

00:31:55.230 --> 00:31:59.410
with light, you can then
kind of attach a molecule.

00:31:59.410 --> 00:32:02.880
In this case I have DNA
put on there in between it.

00:32:02.880 --> 00:32:05.160
And so that's kind of
the rope in my diagram.

00:32:05.160 --> 00:32:08.880
And then you can now move those
trapped beads in the light

00:32:08.880 --> 00:32:11.890
and like kind of stretch them
apart and put a lot of energy

00:32:11.890 --> 00:32:14.950
and try to pull that
DNA so that it breaks.

00:32:14.950 --> 00:32:18.230
And like I said before
with, if you think about

00:32:18.230 --> 00:32:21.203
like a small string versus
a really giant rope,

00:32:22.220 --> 00:32:26.150
the more fragile the DNA is the easier,

00:32:26.150 --> 00:32:27.760
the less energy you're
going to have to put in

00:32:27.760 --> 00:32:29.470
to be able to pull it apart, right.

00:32:29.470 --> 00:32:32.930
Whereas if you have DNA or
protein or some other thing

00:32:32.930 --> 00:32:34.810
that you're interested
in, that's really strong

00:32:34.810 --> 00:32:36.700
and really interacts really strongly.

00:32:36.700 --> 00:32:38.710
You're gonna need to
have a lot more energy

00:32:38.710 --> 00:32:41.723
and a lot more force to be able
to pull it before it breaks.

00:32:42.650 --> 00:32:45.240
And so being able to measure
how much energy it takes

00:32:45.240 --> 00:32:47.810
to be able to break that
thing, that's in the middle

00:32:47.810 --> 00:32:49.680
of your beads or in the
middle of your hands

00:32:49.680 --> 00:32:54.200
in this diagram, really tells
you a lot about how strong

00:32:54.200 --> 00:32:57.390
that DNA, that protein
or whatever molecule

00:32:57.390 --> 00:32:58.740
in between it really is.

00:32:58.740 --> 00:33:01.710
And so you really get
a sense of like, okay,

00:33:01.710 --> 00:33:05.090
this is kind of the properties
of this one molecule

00:33:05.090 --> 00:33:06.640
I'm interested in.

00:33:06.640 --> 00:33:09.950
And so when you do the
pulling, you end up getting

00:33:09.950 --> 00:33:13.620
kind of a graph that looks
like this as the kind of data

00:33:13.620 --> 00:33:15.640
from that pulling experiment.

00:33:15.640 --> 00:33:18.480
And you don't have to
understand anything about this

00:33:18.480 --> 00:33:20.740
other than that, basically the higher up

00:33:20.740 --> 00:33:25.030
like vertically you go is just
a measure of how much energy,

00:33:25.030 --> 00:33:27.760
how hard you're trying to pull into it.

00:33:27.760 --> 00:33:30.170
And that kind of tells you
how much energy it takes

00:33:30.170 --> 00:33:33.880
before your DNA in this case snaps.

00:33:33.880 --> 00:33:37.390
And so that's forced
microscopy, and you can do this

00:33:37.390 --> 00:33:40.050
on a single molecule level,
but what's really cool

00:33:40.050 --> 00:33:45.050
about this is you can also
do add in fluorescence

00:33:45.250 --> 00:33:47.090
microscopy in with this.

00:33:47.090 --> 00:33:50.150
And so that now you can using
fluorescence, the properties

00:33:50.150 --> 00:33:53.100
of fluorescence I talk
about, actually look at

00:33:53.100 --> 00:33:56.210
and be able to track single proteins.

00:33:56.210 --> 00:34:01.060
And so here again, it might
be a kind of a busy image

00:34:01.060 --> 00:34:03.470
but all I really want
everybody to focus on

00:34:03.470 --> 00:34:05.600
is like this image right here.

00:34:05.600 --> 00:34:09.170
And so these are the two beads,
like in the diagram before

00:34:09.170 --> 00:34:11.690
that are kind of, if you think
about it as your handholds

00:34:11.690 --> 00:34:12.960
that are pulling.

00:34:12.960 --> 00:34:15.560
And then in between it, these
little bright green spots

00:34:15.560 --> 00:34:17.750
are actually singular proteins.

00:34:17.750 --> 00:34:20.200
And so again, really thinking
about the size scale of this,

00:34:20.200 --> 00:34:22.460
like you have your cell,
which is like massive,

00:34:22.460 --> 00:34:24.980
and then like a little
tiny protein right here.

00:34:24.980 --> 00:34:26.520
And that's what you're looking at.

00:34:26.520 --> 00:34:29.003
You're looking at a singular protein.

00:34:29.003 --> 00:34:31.510
This is like an incredibly small scale.

00:34:31.510 --> 00:34:35.070
And you're actually going to
be able to track as it moves

00:34:35.070 --> 00:34:38.040
along that rope that you
have in between your beads.

00:34:38.040 --> 00:34:40.710
And so that's what these images
are, is just kind of looking

00:34:40.710 --> 00:34:42.370
at those single proteins, moving along

00:34:42.370 --> 00:34:44.260
and doing their thing.

00:34:44.260 --> 00:34:46.140
And so that is single molecule microscopy

00:34:46.140 --> 00:34:48.420
and that's what I'm currently doing.

00:34:48.420 --> 00:34:51.620
And so I wanted to take
this time now to actually

00:34:51.620 --> 00:34:53.200
give you guys a live demonstration

00:34:53.200 --> 00:34:54.843
of how this microscope works.

00:34:57.910 --> 00:35:00.000
And so before I go into
the live demonstration

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:02.120
I want to take another
minute to just, if anybody

00:35:02.120 --> 00:35:04.720
has any questions or anything
they want to bring up.

00:35:11.360 --> 00:35:12.310
Nope. That's okay.

00:35:12.310 --> 00:35:14.380
Okay, we can get to the fun stuff now

00:35:14.380 --> 00:35:17.720
of how this microscope actually works.

00:35:17.720 --> 00:35:19.270
So now I'll switch to my phone.

00:35:23.715 --> 00:35:25.610
Sorry. Okay.

00:35:25.610 --> 00:35:30.610
So first this is what the microscope,

00:35:30.610 --> 00:35:32.230
so we call it a C-trap.

00:35:32.230 --> 00:35:35.170
And so this is what the C-trap looks like.

00:35:35.170 --> 00:35:37.550
And this is the instrument
we use to be able to look

00:35:37.550 --> 00:35:39.810
at singular molecules.

00:35:39.810 --> 00:35:43.157
And you'll notice it's on
this really special table,

00:35:43.157 --> 00:35:44.380
kind of has all these holes in it.

00:35:44.380 --> 00:35:46.400
And so this is actually an airsoft table.

00:35:46.400 --> 00:35:48.170
And you can think about it kind of like

00:35:48.170 --> 00:35:50.560
if you guys have played air
hockey, there's just a lot

00:35:50.560 --> 00:35:53.470
