00:35:53.470 --> 00:35:56.120
And this is important
because we want to minimize

00:35:56.120 --> 00:35:58.980
shaking motions on the microscope.

00:35:58.980 --> 00:36:02.500
And so this is kind of the
little black box and magic

00:36:02.500 --> 00:36:04.800
fluoroscope, but I'll
open it up for you guys.

00:36:06.090 --> 00:36:09.500
And just exactly like I
said, the different parts

00:36:09.500 --> 00:36:11.070
of this bright field microscope,

00:36:11.070 --> 00:36:13.570
all of that is contained in here too.

00:36:13.570 --> 00:36:16.930
And so if you look really
closely here you'll see

00:36:16.930 --> 00:36:20.120
where the objective is,
that middle part there.

00:36:20.120 --> 00:36:23.130
This glass thing, that is
where you put your sample in.

00:36:23.130 --> 00:36:25.620
So just like the glass
microscope scope slide

00:36:25.620 --> 00:36:26.700
I showed you guys earlier.

00:36:26.700 --> 00:36:28.960
And then this top part
is your light source.

00:36:28.960 --> 00:36:32.170
And so you can see this is
where the laser comes through.

00:36:32.170 --> 00:36:35.000
And all these little tubings you see here

00:36:35.000 --> 00:36:39.830
are actually connected to syringes.

00:36:39.830 --> 00:36:44.130
So syringes that you can put your samples

00:36:44.130 --> 00:36:47.490
that you're interested in
looking at, and they will flow

00:36:47.490 --> 00:36:50.623
into the glass side and then
you're able to visualize it.

00:36:51.530 --> 00:36:54.510
And so I have this prepped
already with some samples

00:36:54.510 --> 00:36:55.533
