00:39:35.940 --> 00:39:38.363
- [Jeanne] Rochelle,
what do you use this for?

00:39:39.575 --> 00:39:40.910
- [Rochelle] Yeah, sorry.

00:39:40.910 --> 00:39:43.840
I forgot to talk about my actual project.

00:39:43.840 --> 00:39:47.630
So I am actually studying,
oh, so you can start seeing

00:39:47.630 --> 00:39:49.330
beads getting caught right now.

00:39:49.330 --> 00:39:53.590
And so I study things,
questions involving chromatin.

00:39:53.590 --> 00:39:57.200
And so what chromatin is are actually DNA

00:39:57.200 --> 00:40:01.270
and protein complexes, because
your DNA doesn't just exist

00:40:01.270 --> 00:40:03.420
as bare DNA in the cell.

00:40:03.420 --> 00:40:06.149
It, sorry, I'll switch.

00:40:06.149 --> 00:40:09.670
Also exists as DNA that
it's usually wrapped around

00:40:09.670 --> 00:40:11.290
different kinds of proteins.

00:40:11.290 --> 00:40:13.380
And there's actually a
really complicated level

00:40:13.380 --> 00:40:15.760
of regulation of like a whole
bunch of different proteins,

00:40:15.760 --> 00:40:18.610
make sure that your DNA,
your genes and your DNA

00:40:18.610 --> 00:40:21.460
get expressed only at certain
times versus other times.

00:40:21.460 --> 00:40:24.400
And they don't get expressed
like when they shouldn't be.

00:40:24.400 --> 00:40:28.760
And so my project kind of
is looking at how do some

00:40:28.760 --> 00:40:31.800
of these proteins really
coordinate that regulation

00:40:31.800 --> 00:40:36.240
and how do they coordinate in
like a very physical manner.

00:40:36.240 --> 00:40:38.240
So do they do it by literally making sure

00:40:38.240 --> 00:40:40.110
the DNA gets scrunched
up and is like solid

00:40:40.110 --> 00:40:43.150
and can't get opened up
physically or do they do it

00:40:43.150 --> 00:40:46.870
by like making chromatin,
making kind of DNA complexes

00:40:46.870 --> 00:40:49.660
that are a little bit
more easy to be opened up

00:40:49.660 --> 00:40:51.120
and be able to be accessed

00:40:51.120 --> 00:40:53.623
by things that then express genes.

00:40:55.090 --> 00:40:56.780
- [Jeanne] Cool. Thank you.

00:40:56.780 --> 00:40:57.880
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:40:57.880 --> 00:41:01.300
So now we are finally catching
some beads, so you see,

00:41:01.300 --> 00:41:02.540
this is one of the plastic beads

00:41:02.540 --> 00:41:05.160
that's actually getting
caught by the laser.

00:41:05.160 --> 00:41:07.990
And I've just moved from my bead channel

00:41:07.990 --> 00:41:09.820
up to the DNA channel.

00:41:09.820 --> 00:41:13.150
And so that's where I have DNA on my rope

00:41:13.150 --> 00:41:14.600
kind of waiting around.

00:41:14.600 --> 00:41:16.633
And literally all I do now, you see,

00:41:16.633 --> 00:41:19.190
is that I kind of just using my joystick,

00:41:19.190 --> 00:41:22.010
move back and forth and try to catch DNA.

00:41:22.010 --> 00:41:24.770
And I keep an eye out on this window,

00:41:24.770 --> 00:41:27.910
and what this window is showing
me is just like that diagram

00:41:27.910 --> 00:41:29.560
I had originally showed you guys.

00:41:30.650 --> 00:41:33.400
This keeps track of how
much energy is being put

00:41:33.400 --> 00:41:36.330
into my bead and rope system.

00:41:36.330 --> 00:41:38.920
And so as I'm moving, you can see

00:41:38.920 --> 00:41:40.060
that I'm starting to go up.

00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:42.190
That means I'm trying to
pull harder and harder.

00:41:42.190 --> 00:41:44.250
So that means that I got something caught

00:41:44.250 --> 00:41:46.020
in between my two beads.

00:41:46.020 --> 00:41:49.260
And since I got something
caught, that means I can move up

00:41:49.260 --> 00:41:52.260
to kind of a channel
that has nothing in it.

00:41:52.260 --> 00:41:56.207
I can close all of my,
we call them bowels.

00:41:57.740 --> 00:41:59.350
They're basically these tubes.

00:41:59.350 --> 00:42:02.530
So they all have openings
that open and close

00:42:02.530 --> 00:42:04.860
to allow your sample
to flow through or not.

00:42:04.860 --> 00:42:07.033
And so I closed them so that there's not,

00:42:08.050 --> 00:42:12.650
basically there's no like,
the fluid isn't moving,

00:42:12.650 --> 00:42:15.960
basically like when that will
kind of blow something away.

00:42:15.960 --> 00:42:18.750
And so they're closed,
I'm in a buffer channel.

00:42:18.750 --> 00:42:23.750
And then now I can pull and
see and kind of look at, okay,

00:42:26.100 --> 00:42:27.670
how much energy do I have to put in

00:42:27.670 --> 00:42:29.400
before I see something break?

00:42:29.400 --> 00:42:32.170
And so right now you can see
I'm putting in a lot of energy,

00:42:32.170 --> 00:42:34.410
I'm pulling a lot, pulling a lot.

00:42:34.410 --> 00:42:37.130
And then that dip down that just happened.

00:42:37.130 --> 00:42:38.740
That was something breaking.

00:42:38.740 --> 00:42:40.610
And so then I can take
that point and be like,

00:42:40.610 --> 00:42:43.520
okay it broke here at this much energy

00:42:43.520 --> 00:42:44.820
that I put into the system.

00:42:44.820 --> 00:42:48.023
And so then that tells me kind
of how strong that DNA was.

00:42:49.410 --> 00:42:53.640
So yeah, that's kind of
just the general gist of

00:42:53.640 --> 00:42:56.183
kind of what I do down
here in the basement.

00:42:57.650 --> 00:42:59.950
So with that, I'll stop this video

00:43:01.800 --> 00:43:03.130
and come back to the laptop.

00:43:03.130 --> 00:43:06.680
And that's kind of what I have
to show to you guys I think,

00:43:06.680 --> 00:43:09.910
kind of almost stopped there,
right around this proper time.

00:43:09.910 --> 00:43:13.113
But totally, if anybody has
any questions or anything.

00:43:17.160 --> 00:43:20.320
- [Jeanne] We see a lot of that
was super cool and amazing.

00:43:20.320 --> 00:43:22.440
Yeah. Really awesome.

00:43:22.440 --> 00:43:24.130
- I can see the chat
blink and I'm like, oh,

00:43:24.130 --> 00:43:24.963
I hope it's good.

00:43:24.963 --> 00:43:27.173
And I hope it's not like,
oh, this is terrible.

00:43:29.040 --> 00:43:30.470
- [Jeanne] Yeah, does
anybody have any questions,

00:43:30.470 --> 00:43:31.930
we'd love to hear from you please?

00:43:31.930 --> 00:43:35.820
You know, no question is
too big or too small to ask.

00:43:35.820 --> 00:43:38.420
You're welcome to ask even the questions

00:43:38.420 --> 00:43:42.403
that you feel might be silly
or whatever, go for it.

00:43:43.300 --> 00:43:45.240
- So I think I see this one, right?

00:43:45.240 --> 00:43:47.340
What do you enjoy most
while working in the lab?

00:43:47.340 --> 00:43:49.603
Oh, big loaded question.

00:43:51.370 --> 00:43:55.090
I think I will be honest, using the C-trap

00:43:55.090 --> 00:43:58.100
sometimes is the most
frustrating part of my day

00:43:58.100 --> 00:44:00.930
because a lot of times
it doesn't work as nicely

00:44:00.930 --> 00:44:02.631
as what just happened.

00:44:02.631 --> 00:44:05.440
And so I will actually say
that it's not even doing

00:44:05.440 --> 00:44:08.170
the experiments that's the
best part of being in a lab.

00:44:08.170 --> 00:44:10.420
It's actually being able to be with people

00:44:10.420 --> 00:44:13.010
and really have this really
great exchange of ideas

00:44:13.010 --> 00:44:15.260
and science and be able
to learn from people

00:44:15.260 --> 00:44:17.360
and then also teach other people.

00:44:17.360 --> 00:44:19.510
And so I think that's
what I enjoy the most

00:44:19.510 --> 00:44:21.690
is just being able to be in an environment

00:44:21.690 --> 00:44:23.210
where everybody is super passionate

00:44:23.210 --> 00:44:24.370
about the same thing I am.

00:44:24.370 --> 00:44:27.700
And we all just like, love
learning about biology

00:44:27.700 --> 00:44:30.280
and being able to talk
and like kind of hang out

00:44:30.280 --> 00:44:31.130
with people here.

00:44:31.980 --> 00:44:34.530
Oh, the name of the machine is here.

00:44:34.530 --> 00:44:35.610
How about I'll just write it.

00:44:35.610 --> 00:44:38.730
So this machine in
particular is called C-trap

00:44:38.730 --> 00:44:43.730
but in general you can also,
if you want to learn more,

00:44:44.170 --> 00:44:45.810
you can look up optical tweezers.

00:44:45.810 --> 00:44:49.310
So those are actually
what, that's the principle

00:44:49.310 --> 00:44:52.363
of using light to trap things,
to grab things basically.

00:45:04.584 --> 00:45:06.420
- [Jeanne] Yeah. Great.

00:45:06.420 --> 00:45:07.940
I love this question from Naomi.

00:45:07.940 --> 00:45:10.253
Are there any disadvantages to the C-trap?

00:45:12.470 --> 00:45:17.070
- Yeah, like I said, sometimes
it's very very frustrating

00:45:17.070 --> 00:45:22.050
to use, but I think
actually some of the biggest

00:45:22.050 --> 00:45:24.970
disadvantages with single
molecule techniques in general

00:45:24.970 --> 00:45:29.560
is that you're really taking
your protein or your molecule

00:45:29.560 --> 00:45:32.180
out from the cell to
be able to look at it.

00:45:32.180 --> 00:45:34.860
And that means you have a lot
of control over that system.

00:45:34.860 --> 00:45:37.370
Like I can manipulate
that DNA however I want.

00:45:37.370 --> 00:45:40.330
And that's really great because
you can't do that in a cell

00:45:40.330 --> 00:45:43.980
but then at the same time, that
means it's in a environment

00:45:43.980 --> 00:45:46.060
that's not what it usually is in.

00:45:46.060 --> 00:45:48.730
Right, and so you always have
to be careful that the things

00:45:48.730 --> 00:45:51.660
that you find and you
discover on the C-trap

00:45:51.660 --> 00:45:53.900
really happen actually in the cell.

00:45:53.900 --> 00:45:55.180
Otherwise you don't really have

00:45:55.180 --> 00:45:57.370
any sort of biological relevance.

00:45:57.370 --> 00:46:00.680
Basically that like what
you look at is is only true

00:46:00.680 --> 00:46:02.540
when you isolate it and it's not actually

00:46:02.540 --> 00:46:04.720
what's actually happening in your body.

00:46:04.720 --> 00:46:07.920
So I think that's one of
the biggest disadvantages

00:46:07.920 --> 00:46:10.630
but at the same time
you can kind of say that

00:46:10.630 --> 00:46:13.020
about every technique in research.

00:46:13.020 --> 00:46:16.330
Like there's always the
question of biological relevance

00:46:16.330 --> 00:46:17.743
is this actually happening?

00:46:19.430 --> 00:46:21.130
So I think that's the biggest one.

00:46:22.710 --> 00:46:24.880
- [Jeanne] I Think that's
a really great question

00:46:24.880 --> 00:46:26.360
and a really great point.

00:46:26.360 --> 00:46:29.730
And you know, if you were to
tell a story about your science

00:46:29.730 --> 00:46:33.363
you wouldn't just show data
from the C-trap, right?

00:46:34.620 --> 00:46:37.640
How would you kind of, what
other types of techniques

00:46:37.640 --> 00:46:42.560
might you yourself pair with
data from something like this

00:46:42.560 --> 00:46:46.150
to sort of validate the results
so that it wasn't something

00:46:46.150 --> 00:46:47.900
that you saw 'cause if
it was in a test tube,

00:46:47.900 --> 00:46:50.760
but something that actually is alive.

00:46:50.760 --> 00:46:51.990
- Yeah. Great question.

00:46:51.990 --> 00:46:56.070
And I also forgot I had
this whole introduction

00:46:56.070 --> 00:46:58.010
in the beginning I was gonna
do and I think I forgot

00:46:58.010 --> 00:46:59.260
to do all of it.

00:46:59.260 --> 00:47:01.290
But one thing I forgot to mention

00:47:01.290 --> 00:47:03.440
is that I'm actually
in two different labs.

00:47:03.440 --> 00:47:05.810
So one of the labs is
the single molecule lab

00:47:05.810 --> 00:47:08.560
that uses the C-trap and
the other lab actually

00:47:08.560 --> 00:47:12.290
uses frogs to be able to
investigate chromatin.

00:47:12.290 --> 00:47:15.110
And the reason why I
decided to join the two

00:47:15.110 --> 00:47:19.840
is exactly the the problem that
Naomi and Jeanne brought up

00:47:19.840 --> 00:47:24.090
is that I wanted to make sure
I have experience in a system

00:47:24.090 --> 00:47:27.113
that is able to then make
sure that everything I find

00:47:27.113 --> 00:47:28.670
at the single molecule level

00:47:28.670 --> 00:47:31.360
actually happens in an organism.

00:47:31.360 --> 00:47:36.360
And so what I'm trying to
do is pair using frog eggs

00:47:36.740 --> 00:47:39.410
which are also very easily
manipulatable system

00:47:39.410 --> 00:47:41.980
to be able to kind of
verify everything I see

00:47:41.980 --> 00:47:44.360
on the C-trap, in a frog egg.

00:47:44.360 --> 00:47:46.810
And be like, okay, well
at least it's happening

00:47:46.810 --> 00:47:49.333
in this kind of biologically
relevant system.

00:47:51.870 --> 00:47:54.492
- [Jeanne] I'm sorry,
I'm asking more questions

00:47:54.492 --> 00:47:55.840
than you can see.

00:47:55.840 --> 00:48:00.210
Aren't they the largest
cell like cell that exists.

00:48:00.210 --> 00:48:03.930
So you can see a frog
egg with a naked eye.

00:48:03.930 --> 00:48:05.140
Is that true?

00:48:05.140 --> 00:48:06.490
- Yes. Yes.

00:48:06.490 --> 00:48:07.480
I like to think of that.

00:48:07.480 --> 00:48:09.890
They're not as big as a tapioca ball

00:48:09.890 --> 00:48:12.230
but I call them little
like balls basically.

00:48:12.230 --> 00:48:15.610
They're like this big, but
you can totally see them.

00:48:15.610 --> 00:48:17.270
And every time I look at
them, I'm always like, oh,

00:48:17.270 --> 00:48:18.770
I really want like bubble tea.

00:48:20.760 --> 00:48:22.580
- [Jeanne] Well everybody,
anytime you drink bubble tea

00:48:22.580 --> 00:48:24.130
please think frog oocytes.

00:48:25.620 --> 00:48:28.270
- Well, the funny thing
is, so I'm Taiwanese.

00:48:28.270 --> 00:48:30.410
Like all my family is
from Taiwan and in Taiwan

00:48:30.410 --> 00:48:32.240
there's a dessert called frog eggs

00:48:32.240 --> 00:48:34.690
because they're like that
size and they're chewy.

00:48:36.320 --> 00:48:39.210
- [Jeanne] So, Daniel wants to
know if you've ever dissected

00:48:39.210 --> 00:48:43.467
frog eggs or frogs before,
have you dissected frogs?

00:48:43.467 --> 00:48:47.510
- No, so what's kind of nice about the lab

00:48:47.510 --> 00:48:50.020
that uses the frogs is
that we don't actually kill

00:48:50.020 --> 00:48:50.990
the frogs at all.

00:48:50.990 --> 00:48:52.660
We only harvest their eggs.

00:48:52.660 --> 00:48:56.260
And so I think that's kind of
nice because I feel really bad

00:48:56.260 --> 00:48:58.900
about like killing animals
or dissecting them,

00:48:58.900 --> 00:49:01.560
even though I understand like we really do

00:49:01.560 --> 00:49:04.280
sometimes need to do that
to understand biology

00:49:04.280 --> 00:49:07.947
and help with like curing diseases.

00:49:07.947 --> 00:49:10.930
But I just, personally if I
can get away from doing it,

00:49:10.930 --> 00:49:11.840
I would.

00:49:11.840 --> 00:49:14.640
So I haven't dissected any frogs.

00:49:14.640 --> 00:49:16.480
I think the last time
I dissected something

00:49:16.480 --> 00:49:19.910
was in high school in my biology class,

00:49:19.910 --> 00:49:21.243
we dissected a fetal pig.

00:49:22.100 --> 00:49:23.700
So it's been a long time for me.

00:49:26.360 --> 00:49:27.843
- [Jeanne] Yeah, same.

00:49:28.770 --> 00:49:33.240
Other questions before we end our session

00:49:33.240 --> 00:49:35.880
with Rochelle and the basement microscope.

00:49:35.880 --> 00:49:37.300
- Yeah.

00:49:37.300 --> 00:49:41.060
- [Jeanne] C-trap reminds me
of some sort of like, you know,

00:49:41.060 --> 00:49:45.363
dance hall kind of like secret programs.

00:49:46.470 --> 00:49:48.391
- I was thinking, it's like,

00:49:48.391 --> 00:49:51.188
'cause this is all like
soundproofing, I think.

00:49:51.188 --> 00:49:55.070
So I'm like, well, I could
do a recording session here.

00:49:55.070 --> 00:50:00.070
- [Jeanne] So we have another
question from Yocelin,

00:50:00.530 --> 00:50:03.180
please excuse me if I
pronounced your name incorrect.

00:50:04.460 --> 00:50:06.263
Why is your research important?

00:50:07.420 --> 00:50:08.253
- Another, wow.

00:50:08.253 --> 00:50:10.340
You guys are asking really
the big questions here.

00:50:10.340 --> 00:50:13.290
I feel like I'm like defending
myself against my committee.

00:50:14.880 --> 00:50:16.610
No, I think this is a
really great question

00:50:16.610 --> 00:50:18.660
and that's something
definitely every scientist

00:50:18.660 --> 00:50:20.730
needs to really be constantly considering

00:50:20.730 --> 00:50:24.700
as they do research of
like, you know, making sure

00:50:24.700 --> 00:50:27.700
you can convey why what
you're doing matters

00:50:27.700 --> 00:50:29.470
and why it's really important.

00:50:29.470 --> 00:50:33.690
And so kind of my answer to
it is I'm really interested

00:50:33.690 --> 00:50:36.780
in things called like basic
bile, like basic science

00:50:36.780 --> 00:50:40.160
which is really just
understanding how do things work

00:50:40.160 --> 00:50:43.740
and not necessarily have
much of a disease relevancy.

00:50:43.740 --> 00:50:45.830
Like it can't be translated into a cure

00:50:45.830 --> 00:50:47.450
for something as easily.

00:50:47.450 --> 00:50:49.360
But I think that's still
really really important

00:50:49.360 --> 00:50:52.710
because to be able to cure, fix something,

00:50:52.710 --> 00:50:55.230
you really have to understand
how it works, right?

00:50:55.230 --> 00:50:59.610
First of all, and even though
DNA and chromatin biology

00:50:59.610 --> 00:51:01.810
is like a really really well studied field

00:51:01.810 --> 00:51:02.840
and a pretty old field.

00:51:02.840 --> 00:51:04.530
There's still just an amazing amount.

00:51:04.530 --> 00:51:06.940
We really don't understand about like,

00:51:06.940 --> 00:51:08.650
how is your nucleus organized?

00:51:08.650 --> 00:51:10.510
How do different proteins bind on

00:51:10.510 --> 00:51:13.660
and make sure they regulate
gene expression and etc.

00:51:13.660 --> 00:51:16.960
And those all translate
directly into diseases.

00:51:16.960 --> 00:51:20.210
And so they're not proteins,
I'm kind of studying

00:51:20.210 --> 00:51:23.610
and looking at, if you're
deficient or if you have mutated

00:51:23.610 --> 00:51:28.080
versions of them there are
like diseases related to that.

00:51:28.080 --> 00:51:30.200
And then more just generally

00:51:30.200 --> 00:51:34.100
if you have chromatin dysregulation,
so something goes wrong

00:51:34.100 --> 00:51:36.500
in regulating a chromatin that definitely,

00:51:36.500 --> 00:51:38.220
immediately leads to cancer.

00:51:38.220 --> 00:51:40.670
And so there is a direct lead to that too.

00:51:40.670 --> 00:51:45.590
And so my kind of, I
guess, broad overall answer

00:51:45.590 --> 00:51:47.760
to that is I think this
is really important

00:51:47.760 --> 00:51:51.200
because understanding how
your chromatin functions

00:51:51.200 --> 00:51:54.810
then makes it possible to
understand why when it goes wrong

00:51:54.810 --> 00:51:56.273
it leads to diseases.

00:51:58.220 --> 00:52:00.060
- [Jeanne] And I think we
have, thank you so much.

00:52:00.060 --> 00:52:02.620
And then we have another
question from Naomi

00:52:02.620 --> 00:52:06.113
about the user-friendliness of the C-trap.

00:52:08.090 --> 00:52:10.410
- So I think just in
general, it's actually,

00:52:10.410 --> 00:52:11.270
it's not that bad.

00:52:11.270 --> 00:52:13.380
I think I have definitely
worked with microscopes

00:52:13.380 --> 00:52:18.000
a lot more harder to initially get onto.

00:52:18.000 --> 00:52:19.050
And part of that is because

00:52:19.050 --> 00:52:20.850
this is already a commercial setup.

00:52:20.850 --> 00:52:23.200
And so the company has
already took the hard part

00:52:23.200 --> 00:52:25.450
and made it as user-friendly as possible.

00:52:25.450 --> 00:52:29.290
And so literally, basically
the only things you kind of,

00:52:29.290 --> 00:52:31.450
sorry, I'm connected to
three different things

00:52:31.450 --> 00:52:35.270
are just your joystick which,
I mean, I think a lot of us

00:52:35.270 --> 00:52:37.730
especially the younger
generation knows how joysticks

00:52:37.730 --> 00:52:39.860
kind of work and can use it.

00:52:39.860 --> 00:52:43.100
And then you just kind of,
there's only a just like a couple

00:52:43.100 --> 00:52:43.933
of buttons you can do.

00:52:43.933 --> 00:52:47.060
Sorry, I think me showing
you this makes it seem

00:52:47.060 --> 00:52:50.340
more overwhelming than it is,
but literally all I ever do

00:52:50.340 --> 00:52:52.530
is I just move from these three things.

00:52:52.530 --> 00:52:55.230
So I go, I'm at my buffer right now.

00:52:55.230 --> 00:52:58.350
And then I go like,
okay, I'm going to DNA,

00:52:58.350 --> 00:52:59.860
I'm going to beads.

00:52:59.860 --> 00:53:01.713
And then I just move around a lot.

00:53:02.580 --> 00:53:04.610
And then that's kind of
like all you really do

00:53:04.610 --> 00:53:05.443
with the microscope.

00:53:05.443 --> 00:53:08.320
So it's actually really, really
easy to learn and pick up.

00:53:09.610 --> 00:53:12.260
- [Jeanne] Do you think that
setting up your experiment

00:53:12.260 --> 00:53:14.840
is sort of like learning how
to think about and set up

00:53:14.840 --> 00:53:16.410
your experiment is more difficult

00:53:16.410 --> 00:53:18.950
than actually using the equipment?

00:53:18.950 --> 00:53:21.470
- Yeah, I think definitely
the hardest part of research

00:53:21.470 --> 00:53:23.850
is always experimental design.

00:53:23.850 --> 00:53:27.450
Because you want to make
sure have enough controls

00:53:27.450 --> 00:53:30.800
built in so that okay, if I
see something interesting,

00:53:30.800 --> 00:53:33.650
is it actually interesting
or is it because of something

00:53:33.650 --> 00:53:36.880
I accidentally did to
manipulate the environment

00:53:36.880 --> 00:53:37.980
or vice versa?

00:53:37.980 --> 00:53:40.880
If I don't see anything,
does that mean it didn't work

00:53:40.880 --> 00:53:43.810
or did it mean that maybe I
just forgot to add something in.

00:53:43.810 --> 00:53:45.747
